# What must you know about rook endings?
teaches: ["endgames/rook-endings"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "Why does the rook belong behind the passed pawn? How do you hold Philidor's position and win Lucena's?"
next: {"title":"Bishop or knight in the ending?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/25-bishop-or-knight-in-the-ending.cast"}

## Warm-up: the square and the opposition
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 8/8/8/6k1/1P5p/8/8/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [b5]}
      - {fen: 8/4k3/4P3/5K2/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 1, moves: [Kd6, Kf6, Kc6, e7, Kd7, Kf7, Kd6, e8=Q], flip: true}
      - {fen: 8/4k3/4P3/5K2/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 1, moves: [Ke8], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: rook endings, the endings you'll meet most often. First, two warm-ups from yesterday's pawn endings.
    draw position

B: Black's pawn is running down the h-file. I'd chase it: king to h2.
    highlight piece_h4

A: Your king already stands inside that pawn's square. Count the square of your own pawn instead.
    highlight piece_b4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win the race." widget chess line true hints ["Count the square of your own pawn.","Push the b-pawn now."] themes ["square rule"] voice a teaches ["endgames/square-rule"]
        right: Pawn to b5. The pawn's square shrinks, Black's king is now outside it, and your own king still stops the h-pawn.
        wrong: Draw the square from your b-pawn to the eighth rank. Where is Black's king?

B: So I was chasing a pawn I'd already caught.
    highlight piece_g1

A: Next, you defend. The board turns round: you're Black.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: White's king is coming. I'd step toward it: king to d6.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Then White's king steps to f6,
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.2

A: and the pawn walks through to e8.
    animate plies_shown 8 duration 3.5 easing linear

    pause 0.5

    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Save the draw." widget chess line true hints ["Keep your king in front of the pawn.","Where does your king answer when White's comes to f6?"] themes ["opposition"] voice a teaches ["endgames/opposition"]
        right: King to e8, straight back. When White's king comes to f6, yours answers on f8: you keep the opposition, and it's a draw.
        wrong: Off the pawn's file, White's king gets past you. Stay in front of the pawn.

B: Straight back, in front of the pawn. Not toward the king.
    highlight piece_e8

A: The square and the opposition: you need both, because when the rooks come off, a rook ending becomes a pawn ending.
    highlight piece_e8 piece_e6

## Three positions to know
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: R7/P5k1/8/8/8/8/r7/6K1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Kf7, Rh8, Rxa7, Rh7+], highlights: [g7, h7]}
      - {fen: 4k3/R7/1r6/4PK2/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 1, moves: [Rb8, Kf6], flip: true}
      - {fen: 4k3/R7/1r6/4PK2/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 1, moves: [Rc6, e6, Rc1, Kf6, Rf1+], flip: true}
      - {fen: 3K4/3P2k1/8/8/8/8/2r5/5R2 w - - 0 1, moves: [Ke7, Re2+, Kd6, Rd2+, Ke6, Re2+]}
      - {fen: 3K4/3P2k1/8/8/8/8/2r5/5R2 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rf4, Rc1, Ke7, Re1+, Kd6, Rd1+, Ke6, Re1+, Kd5, Rd1+, Rd4]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: rookend
      title: Rook and pawn versus rook endgame
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: Endings with rooks and pawns are the most common type of endgame, occurring in about 8 to 10 percent of all games.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rook_and_pawn_versus_rook_endgame
    - id: tarrasch
      title: Tarrasch rule
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: Siegbert Tarrasch stated in his book on St. Petersburg 1914 that rooks belong behind passed pawns, both one's own and the opponent's.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarrasch_rule
    - id: philidor
      title: Philidor position
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Analysed by François-André Danican Philidor in 1777: the defender keeps the rook on the third rank until the pawn advances, then checks the king from behind.'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philidor_position
    - id: lucena
      title: The Lucena Position
      authors: Edward Winter (Chess Notes)
      year: 2026
      finding: The position is not in Lucena's 1497 book; its earliest printed source is Salvio's Il Puttino (1634), which credits Scipione Genovino; the misattribution goes back to the Handbuch (1880) and Berger.
      url: https://chesshistory.com/winter/extra/lucena.html

    card title "Three positions to know"

A: Now the rooks stay on. Rook endings come up in about one game in ten, more than any other ending.
    draw position

A: First: White is a pawn up, and the pawn stands on the seventh.
    highlight piece_a7

B: Then White wins. It's one step from queening.
    point at.ref sq_a8 gesture circle

A: Look at White's rook: it stands in front of its own pawn, and it can't leave a8 without losing it.
    highlight piece_a8

A: But Black's rook stands behind the pawn, free to check. With care, it's a draw.
    highlight piece_a2

B: Why doesn't Black's king just go and win the pawn?
    highlight piece_g7

A: King to f7 walks into rook to h8. Now the pawn threatens to queen,
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.2

    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.2

A: and if the rook takes it,
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.2

A: rook h7 check skewers the king and the rook.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.2

    pause 0.4

    animate plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

A: So Black's king stays on g7 or h7.
    draw hl_g7 hl_h7 parallel true

    erase hl_g7 hl_h7

    portrait tarrasch cites ["tarrasch"] x 908 y 540 width 130 of "Siegbert Tarrasch" cameo true

A: The German master Siegbert Tarrasch made it a rule: rooks belong behind passed pawns, your own and the enemy's.
    highlight piece_a2

    erase tarrasch

A: Second: the draw every defender needs,
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: analysed by François-André Philidor in 1777. We're Black now, a pawn down.
    portrait philidor cites ["philidor"] x 908 y 540 width 130 of "François Philidor" cameo true

B: I'd bring my rook to the back rank, b8.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

    erase philidor

A: Then White's king steps onto the sixth rank, and Black is lost.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Philidor's rule: keep the rook on the sixth rank,
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: counting from the side with the pawn, and the king can't come in.
    highlight piece_b6

A: It just waits along the sixth.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

B: So White pushes the pawn to e6.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Now the rook goes to the far end.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: With the pawn on e6, the king has no shelter from checks behind it.
    highlight piece_e6

B: King to f6, and he threatens mate on a8.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.3

    pause 0.3

    ask "Hold the draw." widget chess line true hints ["Mate on a8 is threatened. Look for a check.","Your rook, from behind the king."] themes ["rook ending","defence"] voice a
        right: Rook to f1, check, from behind. The checks keep coming, and Philidor's position is a draw.
        wrong: A quiet move allows mate on a8. Which move comes with check?

A: Third: now you have the pawn.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: The board turns back to White, and your rook cuts Black's king off.
    highlight piece_f1

A: This is the Lucena position, named after a Spanish book of 1497, though it isn't in it. It first appears in print in Salvio's book of 1634.
    highlight piece_d8 piece_d7

B: Easy: king out, then queen. King to e7.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Here that even works, but slowly: the checks chase your king back and forth.
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 4.5

A: The method that works every time is a bridge:
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: rook to the fourth rank.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Then the king walks out, down toward the rook.
    animate plies_shown 9 duration 4.5

B: Another check. Where does it end?
    animate plies_shown 10 duration 1.3

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find the win." widget chess line true accept ["Ke6","Kc6"] hints ["Your rook on the fourth rank is ready.","Block on d4."] themes ["rook ending"] voice a
        right: Rook to d4 blocks the check: that's the bridge, the method that always works. The checks are over, and the pawn queens.
        wrong: Running with the king only brings the next check. Put something between the king and the rook.

A: So: defend with the rook on the sixth, then check from behind. Attack by cutting the king off, then build the bridge.
    highlight piece_d4 piece_d7

## Your turn: rook endings
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Four for four. Philidor would nod.
    good: Most of them. The misses come back until they stick.
    poor: Rook endings take practice. The misses come back.
    none: The positions will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 8/p4p1p/2k4r/8/8/8/5PPP/R5K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Ra6+, Kd5, Rxh6]}
      - {fen: 4k3/4r2R/8/3K4/3P4/8/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxe7+, Kxe7]}
      - {fen: 5R2/8/8/8/8/2kp4/7r/3K4 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rc8+]}
      - {fen: 4R3/8/8/2r5/4k3/8/1K2p3/8 b - - 0 1, moves: [Re5], flip: true}
      - {fen: 4k3/R7/1r6/4PK2/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 1, flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you know the three positions: your turn. Four puzzles, then a game against the computer.
    draw position

B: Black's pawn on a7 is loose. I'd take it.
    highlight piece_a7

A: Checks first. There's a check that wins more.
    highlight piece_a1

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Look at every check.","Which check lines up the king and the rook?"] themes ["skewer","rook ending"] voice a teaches ["tactics/skewer","endgames/rook-endings"]
        right: Rook to a6, check. The king steps away, and the rook on h6 falls: a skewer along the sixth rank.
        wrong: The pawn can wait. Which check puts the king and the rook on one line?

A: Next, a pawn up, rooks facing each other.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: I'd keep the rooks on. More pieces, more chances.
    highlight piece_h7

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find the win." widget chess line true hints ["Count it to the end: what is left after a trade?","Your king already stands in front of its pawn."] themes ["opposition","conversion"] voice a teaches ["endgames/opposition","endgames/conversion"]
        right: Rook takes e7, check, and the king takes back: a won pawn ending, with your king in front of its pawn. Keep the rooks, and the best you get is a draw.
        wrong: With the rooks on, Black holds. What does a trade leave?

A: Now you defend, as White.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Black's pawn is on d3, and Black threatens rook h1, mate.
    highlight piece_h2

    pause 0.3

    ask "Hold the draw." widget chess line true hints ["A quiet move allows mate. Look for a check.","Check the king from behind."] themes ["rook ending","defence"] voice a
        right: Rook to c8, check, from behind, like Philidor's rook. Every other move loses.
        wrong: Mate on h1 is coming. Which move comes with check?

A: Last puzzle: you're Black, with the pawn. The board turns round.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Your king has already walked out, and the checks have started.
    highlight piece_e4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find the win." widget chess line true hints ["Your rook is ready on your fourth rank, counting from your pawn's side.","Block on e5."] themes ["rook ending"] accept ["Kd3","Kf3"] voice a
        right: Rook to e5 blocks the check: the bridge, the method that always works, and the pawn queens.
        wrong: Running with the king only brings the next check. Put something in the way.

A: Now play it out. Philidor's position, Black to move:
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: hold the draw for fifteen moves.
    explore activity vs_computer goal.kind hold goal.within 15 store philidor

    hide position

A: In a rook ending, the rook's square decides it: behind the passed pawn,
    text c_behind "behind the pawn" x 500 y 500 font_size 52

A: on the sixth to defend,
    text c_sixth "sixth: defend" x 500 y 410 font_size 52

A: on the fourth to build a bridge.
    text c_fourth "fourth: bridge" x 500 y 320 font_size 52

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 200 font_size 44

## Where next
end_page: true

Next: Bishop or knight in the ending?
